>Wow. You did that without recourse to iconv. I'm impressed!

The first 256 positions of Unicode are exactly ISO 8859-1. For the map
symbols, I used a lookup table. UTF-8 is then encoded thus:

    U+0000-U+007F:    0xxxxxxx
    U+0080-U+07FF:    110xxxxx 10xxxxxx
    U+0800-U+FFFF:    1110xxxx 10xxxxxx 10xxxxxx
    U+10000-U+10FFFF: 11110xxx 10xxxxxx 10xxxxxx 10xxxxxx

where the high bits go into the first byte sent.

>Oh, thanks for the tip. I'll have to check if my WIN32CON build is clean  
>there. I'm
>using SetConsoleOutputCP() to switch between map and text.

Another tip: use GetVersion() to determine if you have 95-based or NT-based
Windows, and use the Unicode functions on NT-based; this will give you
support for all of ISO 8859-1 and -15, and all map symbols. It also fixes
bug W343-3. (WriteConsoleOutputCharacterA is broken on NT-based Windows,
except in locales where the default CP is 437 or 850.)

